  "Source": "P. W. Baumeister, \"Optical Tunneling and Its Applications to Optical Filters,\" Appl. Opt. 6, 897-904 (1967). doi:10.1364/AO.6.000897 - Table I column 'Fig. 6' (p. 899); Fig. 6, Fig. 7 and text p. 900",
  "Notes": "Fabry-Perot tunnel layer (FTR) filter: two n=1.38 tunnel layers around an n=2.30 spacer, used at 60 deg in glass of index 1.72; strongly polarizing narrow passbands\nSpecification: None stated (illustrative design).\nReported: Fig. 6: one passband per polarization in 0.4-0.7 um, both with peak T ~1; p passband near 0.48 um and s passband near 0.58 um (read from plot), separated by more than 1000 A (text p. 900). Angle shift: when the external angle changes by +1.72 deg (Fig. 7, ~ +1 deg internal), the p passband shifts 85 A toward the blue (~50 A/deg, text p. 900).\nNotes: Table I: layers 1 and 3 physical thickness 0.18 um, index 1.38; layer 2 'quarter-wave optical thickness' 1.837 um, index 2.30 (thicknesses in microns). Interpreted as: layer 2 is one quarter wave at 1.837 um (n d = 0.45925 um, d = 199.674 nm), coded as 1 QWOT at reference_nm 1837. The alternative reading (optical thickness 1.837 um) gives several orders in 0.4-0.7 um and does not match Fig. 6/8. Angle of incidence is not printed for Fig. 6; 60 deg inside the 1.72 medium (as for Figs. 3, 9, 11) is assumed - own TMM at 60 deg gives p peak T = 1.00 at 0.479 um, s peak T = 1.00 at 0.578 um, and p peak shift 0.4788 -> 0.4689 um for 60 -> 61 deg, consistent with the paper. The design is symmetric so the side facing the light does not matter.\nLayers from Table I column 'Fig. 6' (p. 899); Fig. 6, Fig. 7 and text p. 900; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours p peak at 478.8 nm, s peak at 577.9 nm; paper p near 480 nm, s near 580 nm (Fig. 6).",
